## Before you begin

To get started, install the `weave` package and initialize your project. This step registers your team and project with Weave so that the SDK routes spans to the correct location in the UI.

**Python**

```bash lines theme={"system"}
pip install weave
```

Replace `[YOUR-TEAM]` with your CoreWeave Forge team name and `[YOUR-PROJECT]` with your Weights & Biases project name.

```python lines theme={"system"}
import weave

weave.init("[YOUR-TEAM]/[YOUR-PROJECT]")
```

Call `weave.init()` before any `start_conversation()`, `start_turn()`, `start_llm()`, `start_tool()`, or `start_subagent()` call. All agent tracing functions no-op silently when tracing is disabled or the init call is absent, so you can leave instrumentation in production code and control it through configuration.

**TypeScript**

```bash lines theme={"system"}
npm install weave
```

Replace `[YOUR-TEAM]` with your CoreWeave Forge team name and `[YOUR-PROJECT]` with your Weights & Biases project name.

```typescript lines twoslash theme={"system"}
// @noErrors
import * as weave from 'weave';

await weave.init('[YOUR-TEAM]/[YOUR-PROJECT]');
```

Call `weave.init()` before any `startConversation()`, `startTurn()`, `startLLM()`, `startTool()`, or `startSubagent()` call. All agent tracing functions no-op silently when tracing is disabled or the init call is absent, so you can leave instrumentation in production code and control it through configuration.

# Customize Ops

> Learn how to color your Ops for better visibility, how to modify what's logged, and how to control the sampling rate

A Weave Op is a versioned function that automatically logs all Calls. This guide is for developers who use Weave to instrument their code, and shows you how to create Ops, customize how they appear in the Weights & Biases UI, control what data Weave logs, and manage sampling and deletion.

**Python**

To create an Op, decorate a Python function with `weave.op()`.

```python lines theme={"system"}
import weave

@weave.op()
def track_me(v):
    return v + 5

weave.init('intro-example')
track_me(15)
```

Calling an Op creates a new Op version if the code changed from the last call, and logs the function's inputs and outputs.

Functions that you decorate with `@weave.op()` behave normally (without code versioning and tracking) if you don't call `weave.init('your-project-name')` before calling them.

You can [serve](https://docs.coreweave.com/products/wandb/weave/guides/tools/serve) or [deploy](https://docs.coreweave.com/products/wandb/weave/guides/tools/deploy) Ops using the Weave toolbelt.

**TypeScript**

To create an Op, wrap a TypeScript function with `weave.op`.

```typescript twoslash lines theme={"system"}
// @noErrors
import * as weave from 'weave'

function trackMe(v: number) {
    return v + 5
}

const trackMeOp = weave.op(trackMe)
trackMeOp(15)

// You can also do this inline, which may be more convenient
const trackMeInline = weave.op((v: number) => v + 5)
trackMeInline(15)
```

